#{[#edb/ref 17592186050713 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060516} :activity/id #uuid "2f099e95-7371-5eb4-a3d6-9210e6669092" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060996 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the point $( - 1 , 2 )$ lies on the graph of $y = f ( x ).$ What point *must* lie on the graph of $y = - f ( x ) + 3?$ This problem can be solved using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Transform the Point Using Graph Transformations**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2)) = (-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2+3)={\\color{blue}(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Evaluate the Transformed Function**\n\nSince the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x),$ we have that $f(-1) = 2.$\n\nNow, consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x = {\\color{blue}-1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+3 \\\\  & =-(2)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\color{blue}(-1, 1)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$\n\nSo, both methods give the same result." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060998 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." :tutorial/title "Mapping Maxima and Minima"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059436 :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y = f(2x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,6-1) = (1,5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060999 :tutorial/content "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." :tutorial/title "Cases With Vertical Reflection"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061000 :tutorial/content "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n> *If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n> *If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060520}}] :activity/title "Finding Points on Transformed Curves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059878}] [#edb/ref 17592186050699 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069694} :activity/id #uuid "50c688c5-1c42-5a32-a485-ba04863ce7e6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069978 :tutorial/content "To sketch the graph of ![](images/0d/0d56960f9390e8a7fe5c1fe17b9fa8b62fe9f33e052eb295161ce7a368047111.svg) we start with the graph of ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) and stretch it by a scale factor of ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d01107a224cc4a12f05263a7f159c8ad25a04ff061e1eda3045113e6671f0113.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point ![](images/0c/0c733476e5ce2ef628b2dd5447582a6e69de8d045147925a803dcfb6e9834a0c.svg) on ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) is mapped to the point ![](images/c8/c8d810917eef2cc68b3c133405d6aa3fe648c64b6f8e50cc1a0bcc4eb84ba536.svg) on ![](images/62/62dc824c36f1634a45236f35fa955f7b7863996ea26708e7e4295d0a73e258be.svg)\n\n- The point ![](images/c1/c18723d4a2788ff033587f5be97e6a0b50d886a8f74a130c92eb348d2a6498b9.svg) on ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) is mapped to the point ![](images/c9/c9629cfe801a15e75ff1548b053d4507fc7bff739ce77315e64cf5bb7fd6bd21.svg) on ![](images/62/62dc824c36f1634a45236f35fa955f7b7863996ea26708e7e4295d0a73e258be.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068619 :question/id #uuid "d86c6bd2-e528-55ed-bbfc-9f5ecbec024e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5924" :question/problem "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068620 :question/id #uuid "ac8b39e3-a9d0-56fd-8c0b-4d748bedf7d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5926" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb46d056a8d8010eca509543c231c875f4980a21f1af17505fa98c8c64106207.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y = \\dfrac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=-1,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$ This means that $k=-1$ and $b=2,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}+(-1) = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}-1.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-2}-1 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{3-2}-1 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k = 3+2+(-1) = 4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068655 :question/id #uuid "a76e0252-0574-520e-bb5f-43c8faf6a564" :question/math-academy-id "e-8658" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{7}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = \\dfrac{7}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e80865b0f32a5473da27b39537034bcdabee0a6c24032f41413e20518642d3f4.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\dfrac{7}{2x}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7b1fe93de798c0aa91f60f66a2507869dbe837b843b7233972e1a78ee22576a.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069986 :tutorial/content "Similarly, to sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{2x},$ we take the graph of $y=\\dfrac1x$ and stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac12$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/08/08c02826d4c2dff684e00c01e9229e5ef7767f4122d99fcfa8e5d63c254cbd94.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $\\left(1,\\dfrac12\\right)$ on $y=\\dfrac{1}{2x}.$\n\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $\\left(-1,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ on $y=\\dfrac{1}{2x}.$" :tutorial/title "Applying a Stretch With a Scale Factor Less Than One"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069987 :tutorial/content "We can apply stretches to negative reciprocal curves too.\n\nFor example, to sketch the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{3}{2x},$ we start with the graph of $y=-\\dfrac1x$ and stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac32$ parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c439cf9ee016a7cb2335a3aa5be18f0c226e32a3efd83d64b4b0ca20404cc051.png)" :tutorial/title "Applying a Stretch to a Negative Reciprocal Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068656 :question/id #uuid "a18ff1e7-7f87-5cdd-9a25-91a2e9c1dd5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8761" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = -\\dfrac{1}{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{1}{5x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 1 5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{5x}.$\n\n![](images/30/30ace3126ec127b9949ebf599d615020b9770458825d22460e9e7e0b3d5efec3.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069698}}] :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations of Reciprocal Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068982}] [#edb/ref 17592186050714 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735} :activity/id #uuid "ec05fcce-5a67-533c-840a-322806556dbc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111872 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t        x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}}\\[5pt]\n\t        x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}}] :activity/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110825}] [#edb/ref 17592186050705 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232} :activity/id #uuid "e92aac25-8e3c-5b47-9748-65ca8bc64600" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129015 :question/id #uuid "9de92581-00d5-5343-90be-3b5527bfab49" :question/math-academy-id "e-7283" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu =3x-2y, \\quad v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(3x-2y)^2 - y - x = 0$ and the line $x+y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/87/87b44aa661a51ee39011c33c2eb000edcebb127508d45d5819339cf532258f3e.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = 3x-2y, \\qquad v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $3x-2y = u$ and $x+y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2y)^{2}-y-x=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, 4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\, u^2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a3d0211dfbe18e5737b62e8b61cf9308af2735ef827d14bae7b5dbfffaa4faa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = (5)^{-1} = \\dfrac 15.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Area by Transforming to a Type I Region"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130454 :tutorial/content "![](images/8f/8f2d689080add393fad398d45d992e595996a12c3eca60c1c97e9a3e14720ed1.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant (shown above) bounded by the lines\n\n$${\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 6, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=4, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=6.$$\n\nThe area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$ where $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nLooking at the lines that bound our region, we consider the following change of variables:\n\n$$u = {\\color{blue}{3y-x}}, \\qquad v = {\\color{red}{2x+y}}$$\n\nNote that this change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$\n\nWe substitute $u=3y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines: $$\\begin{aligned} & 3y-x=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\ & 3y-x=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = 2x+y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines: $$\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4 \\\\ & 2x+y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 6, \\quad 4 \\leq v \\leq 6\\big\\}$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d55142f7713f3a9bfb93d1b1a383683af397a6edcd7c56a4338f25ab16b83a2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is $$u = 3y-x, \\qquad v = 2x+y.$$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =-7.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is $$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac17.$$ Note that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables: $$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{7}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (6-3)\\cdot (6-4) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836 :question/id #uuid "418bea54-7fb3-5013-b91c-355a45af08e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11151" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\nx = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/e4/e4acf61532998ca5b0768b7edde16be779298c1be15ef11b14472678ba43043a.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/37/3706f0373bb39ebd41d78dd453896548ec03638a656e1fe36604f2a43108ba8e.png)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\quad 2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{x}{3y^2} = -\\dfrac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837 :question/id #uuid "acd76e31-5c7e-5577-a402-f003b3c2e1bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-11154" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves.\n\n$$\ne^y = \\dfrac{5}{e^x}, \\qquad e^y=\\dfrac{10}{e^x}, \\qquad e^x=e^y, \\qquad e^x=2e^y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/f4/f47c1b7ce7e9073a10c2665821a4fc0d5b38ce6ad6c0f79a75a5b203962112cd.png)\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\ne^{x+y} = 5, \\qquad e^{x+y}=10, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 1, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = e^{x+y}, \\qquad v = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=e^{x+y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x+y}=5\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x+y}=10\\,⟹\\,u=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v =e^{x-y}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x-y}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & {e}^{x-y}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 5 \\leq u \\leq 10, \\quad 1 \\leq v \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/105745a82e8f67b086f526b0166e3b442d3eaf02e4df69314ed9d3ffd5fca071.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-2x}=-\\dfrac{1}{2uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}\\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{uv}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Not Given"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130464 :tutorial/content "Given a real interval $D = (a,b)\\subset \\mathbb R,$ the length of this interval can be represented by the integral\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int_a^b \\,\\textrm d x$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x.$$\n\nSuppose we define an invertible function $u = u(x).$ By the change of variables formula, the length of our interval can also be expressed as\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Whenever we perform a change of variables, the domain of integration changes, too. This new integration domain is denoted $\\Delta.$ More technically, $\\Delta$ is the *image* of the $D$ under the action of $u(x).$\n- If we think of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}$ as a fraction, the $\\textrm d u$'s in our length formula appear to \"cancel out,\" leaving only the original $\\textrm d x.$ This makes the change of variables formula easy to remember.\n\nSo, to summarize,\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.$$\n\nSince the length of our interval is simply $b-a,$ it's doubtful that we'll ever use this formula in practice! However, it does indicate how we might use a change of variables to find the *area* of a complex region in two-dimensional space. Let's explore this idea in more detail." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130465 :tutorial/content "![](images/6e/6ee5a19bf58db28574dc82aa3e6adcfd59015777bc503e66b9b9fd427278dcbf.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. The area of this region is given by\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$$\n\nwhere we'll use the convention $\\textrm d A = \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$ when referring to integration with respect to the Cartesian coordinates $x$ and $y.$\n\nThis region looks pretty complicated. In such cases, it's often easier to find an invertible transformation $\\mathbf T$ (i.e., change of variables) whose inverse maps the region $D$ to simpler, preferably rectangular, region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\nSo, rather than computing the required area by integrating with respect to $x$ and $y,$ we can instead perform the transformation and integrate with respect to $u$ and $v.$\n\nTo carry out this process, we apply the **change of variables formula**\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNotice that this formula is analogous to the one we saw earlier for computing the length of an interval. In particular:\n\n- If we consider the Jacobian as a kind of fraction, the $\\partial(u,v)$ and $\\textrm d u\\textrm d v$ parts appear to \"cancel out,\" similar to the previous formula.\n- Note that $\\mathbf T$ could be orientation-reversing. We take the absolute value of the Jacobian to ensure that $D$ and $\\Delta$ have the same orientation.\n\nFinally, note that the change of variables formula assumes that $\\mathbf T$ is a $C^1$ transformation and has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$\n\nOften, the most challenging part is finding a suitable change of variables. The main idea is to get a nice rectangular shape with sides parallel to the axes in the $uv$-plane. In other words, we require a change of variables that reduces the equations of our curves to something like the following: $$u = \\textrm{const}_1, \\quad u = \\textrm{const}_2, \\quad v = \\textrm{const}_3, \\quad v = \\textrm{const}_4$$\n\nLet's look at a concrete example of computing an area using the change of variables formula." :tutorial/title "The Change of Variables Formula"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130199}}] :activity/title "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129343}]}
